WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.820 --> 00:00:02.200
Good morning, John; it's Friday.

00:00:02.200 --> 00:00:06.760
A few weeks ago a company called Visually
emailed me, and was like "Hey Hank.

00:00:06.760 --> 00:00:11.349
If you could do a high-quality, animated video
on any issue in the world, what would you

00:00:11.349 --> 00:00:12.349
choose?".

00:00:12.349 --> 00:00:18.740
Now that was a hard choice, but I went with
incarceration in America, because it is messed

00:00:18.740 --> 00:00:19.740
up.

00:00:19.740 --> 00:00:24.619
Now, crime is also messed up; bad things happen
to good people, and that's terrible, and something

00:00:24.619 --> 00:00:25.869
should be done about it.

00:00:25.869 --> 00:00:30.080
Well, we send people to prison to be punished,
and to prevent them from doing bad things

00:00:30.080 --> 00:00:33.190
again, and to deter others from breaking the
law.

00:00:33.190 --> 00:00:35.980
Punishment, corrections, and deterrence.

00:00:35.980 --> 00:00:40.940
Now we have this habit of thinking of prisoners
as something very external to society; after

00:00:40.940 --> 00:00:45.480
all, there are literal walls between them
and society: walls capped with razor wire

00:00:45.480 --> 00:00:47.790
and watched over by people with guns.

00:00:47.790 --> 00:00:53.059
But millions of prisoners are released each
year - today's prisoners are tomorrow's neighbors.

00:00:53.059 --> 00:00:58.110
So corrections should probably be the most
important piece of the incarceration pie.

00:00:58.110 --> 00:01:01.000
Unfortunately, it is not.

00:01:01.000 --> 00:01:05.309
We are, however, really good at punishment.

00:01:05.309 --> 00:01:09.290
America has about 4% of the world's people
and about 25% of the world's incarcerated

00:01:09.290 --> 00:01:10.290
people.

00:01:10.290 --> 00:01:13.100
We have the highest incarceration rate in
the world.

00:01:13.100 --> 00:01:17.310
Over the last 30 years, that number has skyrocketed,
increasing over 400%.

00:01:17.310 --> 00:01:22.130
41% of American juveniles and young adults
have been arrested by the time they turn 23.

00:01:22.130 --> 00:01:28.140
Children as young as 13 years old have been
sentenced to die in prison, and our prisons

00:01:28.140 --> 00:01:30.210
violate international standards.

00:01:30.210 --> 00:01:34.360
Solitary confinement increases instability
and violence in inmates, and is considered

00:01:34.360 --> 00:01:39.640
by international law to be torture, but in
America, it's not regulated by anyone except

00:01:39.640 --> 00:01:42.870
the prison officials; no judge, no jury.

00:01:42.870 --> 00:01:47.509
Arguably the most devastating form of punishment
we enact in this country, and yet there is

00:01:47.509 --> 00:01:49.530
no appeals process.

00:01:49.530 --> 00:01:51.229
And you think it's hard to get a job in America?

00:01:51.229 --> 00:01:55.090
Well, we make it intentionally more difficult
to get a job once you have a conviction on

00:01:55.090 --> 00:01:57.560
your record, not to mention just live your
life.

00:01:57.560 --> 00:02:01.680
Convicts are ineligible for welfare, student
loans, public housing, food stamps, and are

00:02:01.680 --> 00:02:05.240
often socially disconnected from community
and family support structures.

00:02:05.240 --> 00:02:08.750
So in addition to have high recidivism rates,
they have very high rates of homelessness

00:02:08.750 --> 00:02:10.300
and suicide.

00:02:10.300 --> 00:02:14.840
Somewhere along the way, we started to think
that being tough on crime meant being tough

00:02:14.840 --> 00:02:15.840
on criminals.

00:02:15.840 --> 00:02:18.150
But that's not the same thing.

00:02:18.150 --> 00:02:23.790
Punishment is only one piece of a much larger
crime reduction pie, and it's an expensive

00:02:23.790 --> 00:02:29.560
one; with some institutions paying more than
$100,000 per year per prisoner.

00:02:29.560 --> 00:02:33.780
Long prison sentences have helped to decrease
crime, but no more than 25% of the decrease

00:02:33.780 --> 00:02:38.861
that we've seen can be attributed to incarceration,
and it costs far beyond just dollars: the

00:02:38.861 --> 00:02:43.110
cost is to people, to our country, to communities,
to families, and to ourselves.

00:02:43.110 --> 00:02:47.820
The policy seems to be, if you've committed
a felony, we just give up on you.

00:02:47.820 --> 00:02:52.960
These wars on crime, wars on drugs, they are
wars on people; the smart political move is

00:02:52.960 --> 00:02:57.400
to appear tough on crime because crime is
scary, so we increased minimum sentences,

00:02:57.400 --> 00:03:00.280
we arrested more people, we sent more of them
to prison.

00:03:00.280 --> 00:03:05.140
That's how we looked tough on crime, but the
results are in: it's bad policy!

00:03:05.140 --> 00:03:09.610
It's cruel, it's shortsighted, and to continue
this policy of mass incarceration would be

00:03:09.610 --> 00:03:10.640
foolish.

00:03:10.640 --> 00:03:16.790
We're living inside of a massive $75 billion
per year failed experiment.

00:03:16.790 --> 00:03:22.290
2010 was the first year in nearly 40 years
that the number of incarcerated individuals

00:03:22.290 --> 00:03:24.620
in America did not increase.

00:03:24.620 --> 00:03:28.140
Policy makers are beginning to realize the
magnitude of this failure, but there is a

00:03:28.140 --> 00:03:30.230
long way to go.

00:03:30.230 --> 00:03:38.090
John, I'll see you on Tuesday.

